  "docs.ai.privacy": "With your consent, CV text and images go to Gemini. All eight routed AI roles use Gemini during testing; RunPod is a disabled placeholder. Original files are discarded; extracted text and evidence stay with your profile. Guest profiles are kept for 7 days. Employer employee summaries combine saved CV facts and fixed Holland results into English, Malay and Mandarin in one request, stored by input fingerprint. Company Future uses organisation-scoped learning and exposure data; its AI button caches the three-language analysis for each input and horizon. KPI targets are suggestions, not measured outcomes. Company/Institution Advisor retrieves this account’s organisation snapshot, saved analysis, up to six recent conversation turns and relevant catalogue references. Answers and references are saved in all three languages. A changed horizon or company snapshot requires a fresh analysis; it cannot take autonomous actions.",

  "docs.riasec.items": "30 workplace activities adapted from O*NET Interest Profiler domains, with English, Malay and Mandarin wording. Five items per RIASEC area; response scale 1–5. This adaptation is not the validated Mini-IP.",
  "docs.riasec.scoring": "All 30 unique answers are required by the API and both assessment screens. Each area uses the average of its five answers, rounded to two decimals. Ties follow R, I, A, S, E, C.",
  "docs.riasec.limits": "Skilytics adapted the activities and translations under the O*NET Tools Developer License. Independent psychometric validation remains outstanding. USDOL/ETA has not approved, endorsed or tested the modifications. Interests are never inferred from CV text.",

  "docs.match.weights": "Within skill fit, tools/subject skills count twice as much as basic Human skills. Weights: skills 40, transferable 15, interest 20, preferences 10, feasibility 15. Additional skills may be suggested from reviewed education and experience using catalogue terms; these are inferred, not verified. RIASEC supplies interest fit, not skill evidence. Optional growth ordering uses the reviewed US BLS 2025–2035 SOC snapshot, not Malaysian demand or a new personal-fit score. The team heatmap separates in-profile, inferred, completed-course, missing and not-required evidence; it does not assess proficiency.",
  "docs.match.demand": "Labour demand is not fabricated. Until a verified Malaysian demand source exists it is null and shown as not verified, not zero.",
  "docs.match.feasibility": "When no verified course exists, feasibility is unavailable and gets zero credit. It is not estimated from course durations.",

  "docs.sdg.method": "Course subject themes take priority: climate 13, clean energy 7, health 3, manufacturing/research 9, productivity 8 and inclusion 10. Education 4.4 is a fallback. Roles use explicit title/sector rules: health 3, education 4, engineering/logistics/research 9, green roles 7/12/13, other work 8. These application associations are not UN occupational classifications or measured impact. The first goal has a bold outline; icons are deduplicated by goal. Role and vacancy filters let users choose work themes without changing personal fit scores. Courses can be filtered by theme too. The chosen Matcher theme carries into Pathway: among skill-relevant, affordable courses, a matching goal adds a 15% editorial preference to the planner value (not an impact score). Required courses remain; unmatched gaps are reported. The preference is saved with the plan. All 17 goals are supported when relevant to the subject. Pathway and completion summaries use the selected courses’ actual goal codes, not fixed platform goals. 4.3/4.4 and 8.2/8.5 are platform targets, not a restriction on role or course themes. Each recommendation has an expandable ELI5 explanation: what the listed topic/skills help you do, and why this relates to the global goal. These are deterministic editorial associations, not AI-generated impact evidence. Mobile filters use a dropdown; desktop keeps theme buttons. Completion is not proof of measurable UN impact.",
